A Timeflow represents the complete historical record of a dataset 
having both snapshots and the intervening log data, allowing the 
Delphix Continuous Data Engine to reconstruct the dataset from any 
specific point in time, enabling precise provisioning, refresh, and 
rewind operations.

Both dSources and VDBs have Timeflows. This Timeflow data serves as the basis for all Virtual Database storage within Delphix. 

Timeflow uses two mechanisms to provide point in time provisioning:

    Periodic snapshots of the dSource or VDB

    Transactions Log collection for all time between backups

Snapshots are created either via a schedule policy or generated on demand. Snapshots allow you to choose a point in time from which to provision, refresh, or rollback. 

Intial dSource Snapshot:
    The initial dSource Snapshot creates a snapshot timestamp in the dSource Timeflow.

Data Blocks:
    In Perforce Delphix (part of the DevOps Data Platform), data blocks (or shared data blocks) refer to the underlying storage units of production data that Delphix virtualizes rather than duplicating

Initial VDB Snapshot:
    The initial VDB Snapshot creates a Snapshot timestamp in the VDBs Timeflow

Subsequent Snapshots:
    Subsequent Snapshots capture the state of a dataset at a specific moment in time. 

SnapSync and LogSync and VDB Snapshot:

    For dSources, the dSource snapshots are taken via the Delphix SnapSync service and database logs are collected via the Delphix LogSync service.

    For VDBs, snapshots are taken via the Delphix VDB snapshot service. Database logs are automatically collected if database logging is enabled on VDBs.

SnapSync:
    The standard process for importing data from a source into the Delphix Continuous Data Engine. An initial SnapSync is performed to create a dSource on the Delphix Continuous Data Engine.

LogSync:
    This feature enables the ingestion and retention of more granular (log-based) source change data. This more granular change data allows for VDB point-in-time provision, refresh, or rollback.

VDB Snapshot:
    VDB Snapshots represent the state of a dataset at a specific moment in time. Snapshots are created via scheduled policies or are generated on demand
    






